    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192">

                <pb facs="0464" n="368" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg368"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890g.6363 T 84/241: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-02-12T14:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 12,
                                1926—2 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 12—12:02
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>30. Department’s No. 20 dated February 10. I agree absolutely that we should
                    maintain our established position to participate on an equal basis.</p>
                <p>Positions of the Department of State and of the American Group are not
                    necessarily identical. It seems to me that our efforts should cease when equal
                    participation has been accorded to American nationals. The question of whether
                    or not the American Group accepts the conditions offered, or endeavors to modify
                    the conditions to its advantage, does not, in my opinion, lie within our proper
                    field of representation.</p>
                <p>I perceive no objection to the Department’s suggestion …</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Houghton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
